Branch 4: reflection-and-development
Reflection and Professional Development
Development of Machine-Learning Knowledge

The module expanded my knowledge across several areas.

I developed a stronger understanding of:

correlation and regression;
similarity measures;
perceptrons;
neural networks;
gradient descent;
convolutional neural networks;
recommender systems;
matrix factorisation;
neural collaborative filtering;
validation;
performance metrics;
explainability;
bias;
ethical deployment.

The most important change was recognising that model performance cannot be separated from data quality, validation and use context.

Development Through Tutor Feedback

The Airbnb feedback was an important learning experience.

The group achieved a mark of 66, but the tutor identified limitations in validation, model justification, interpretation and business usefulness.

Initially, I focused on the amount of work completed. After reflection, I understood that academic quality depends on whether the claims are supported by evidence.

The low R² showed that the model had limited ability to explain Airbnb prices. Instead of treating this result as a minor weakness, I learned that it should define the boundaries of the recommendation.

Development Through the Individual Project

The individual recommender-system project was designed in response to the group feedback.

I introduced:

a strong classical baseline;
a temporal split;
a validation set;
a final untouched test set;
cross-validation sensitivity analysis;
prediction and ranking metrics;
explainability;
subgroup diagnostics;
deployment controls.

This represented a more disciplined approach to machine learning.

Development as a Team Member

My team contribution was strongest in:

reviewing work;
finding inconsistencies;
improving technical explanations;
validating conclusions;
providing constructive feedback;
supporting final report quality.

My self-evaluation score was 35 out of 35.

However, I identified three areas for improvement:

delegation;
time management;
early-stage coordination.

In future projects, I will contribute earlier to planning rather than focusing mainly on final-stage review.

Development Actions

I will use a traceability table in future projects to connect:

business problem;
dataset;
target;
features;
preprocessing;
baseline;
advanced model;
validation;
metrics;
limitations;
final decision.

I will also ensure that complex models are compared with strong simpler alternatives.

For tabular data, I will consider:

regularised regression;
random forests;
gradient boosting;
neural networks.

For every model, I will report validation performance and uncertainty rather than relying on one test score.

Professional Impact

The module has strengthened my ability to evaluate machine-learning systems critically.

I no longer define a good model only by accuracy. I also consider:

whether the data are appropriate;
whether the result generalises;
whether the model can be explained;
whether groups are affected differently;
whether user privacy is protected;
whether the model can be monitored;
whether rollback is possible.

This approach is relevant to any organisation using artificial intelligence.

My future professional contribution will combine technical analysis, quality assurance and responsible governance. I will focus not only on what a model can predict but also on whether the complete system can be understood, challenged and safely operated.
